{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/407731-pc-losing-a-social-contested-roll-dilemma","id":407731,"title":"PC losing a social contested roll, dilemma","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-16T07:54:11-08:00","id":407731,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> PC losing a social contested roll, dilemma","content":"Hello,<br /> I'm about to start a campaign after a long break. This period of GMing abstinence has given me a fresh perspective and I started asking myself some questions I was used to ignore.<br /> Here\u2019s one of them:<br /> <br /> During physical struggle it\u2019s quite easy to determine who wins \u2013 the pc or the npc. We just roll and count the lethals. Fair enough.<br /> What should we do with manipulation/presence based struggles, from my own experience if the NPC tries to influence the player with a social roll and wins the result is not always ideal.<br /> If I tell the player to comply, only because they lost a roll and require them to roleplay compliance, they\u2019re usually very unhappy. They do it considering themselves good at what they do, but they later on remember this social confrontation as physical injury. It often happens that they try to get back (even unconsciously) on the npc for this social attack whenever they have an opportunity (especially if it was a supernatural manipulation they were really not aware of).<br /> <br /> All being said basing social interaction on rolls is super unsexy, however we can\u2019t just make the players immune to mental influence due to skills or powers of others. There should be a consequence for having very low composure. How do you fellow GMs deal with this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5073,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5073-casualmoth","user":"CasualMoth","date":". 02-16-2015, 10:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5073-casualmoth","name":"CasualMoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5073_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-16T08:09:11-08:00","id":407769,"title":"","content":"The God Machine has more sophisticated rules on this matter than nWoD core does. I actually made a thread about this very matter a good while ago. The link is below.<br /> <br /> All that being said, what I do now if a player loses a contested social roll is, they can choose to comply with it, or suffer a &quot;Breaking Point.&quot; That is, your character has to roll to maintain their Morality-or-equivalent rating.<br /> <br /> Why? Because even though the character has &quot;free will&quot; of sorts, the social roll has convinced them that doing so is against their better judgment, full stop. The character feels they are done goofing, and might go a bit nuts over that feeling.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/115658-making-social-rolls-against-pcs-matter-without-making-them-mind-control#post115918\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/115658-making-social-rolls-against-pcs-matter-without-making-them-mind-control#post115918\"> Making social rolls against PCs matter without making them mind control - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/115658-making-social-rolls-against-pcs-matter-without-making-them-mind-control#post115918\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/115658-making-social-rolls-against-pcs-matter-without-making-them-mind-control#post115918 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> I've noticed a real issue that I've had trouble resolving on my own, and hopefully this forum will help me in doing that, and perhaps others. In World of Darkness, and RPGs in general, I find it hard to strike a balance between player characters (PCs) being entirely immune to social rolls, and social rolls </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2165-macai","name":"Macai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2165_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"76"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-16T08:12:36-08:00","id":407771,"title":"","content":"Sadly, part of the life about roleplaying games where outcomes are determined by dice is that this type of event can and will happen. Just because a character is good at combat or good at social, does not mean they will have a 100% success rate in those endeavors. From what I have read, it sounds like your players want to be infallible and win every encounter they come across.<br /> <br /> If your players think logically, even the best of people can have a social screw up or be persuaded by an unnaturally good argument from another person. Simply having more dice means that the possibility of failing is less which is an accurate reflection of them being good at what they do.<br /> <br /> Now I for one disagree with social interactions being super unsexy as those types of encounters often lead to some of the best parts of game sessions. It is easy to run a monster of the week campaign where you just go kill things without consequences, but when you are trying to convince someone you belong in that part of the building and they simply refuse to believe you, that is something different altogether.<br /> <br /> As much as I hate to say it, this sounds like something you need to work out between yourself and your players since social interactions are part of the game and that includes both winning and losing them. This goes doubly so if a supernatural power was used. People don't like being told that the character they are playing absolutely believes this man is trustworthy, but this is the World of Darkness and that happens.<br /> <br /> EDIT: I just noticed this was posted under the Changeling section. Everything I said is even more relevant in this case as deception and lies are absolutely rampant in this game line.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":444,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/444-dessembrae","user":"Dessembrae","date":". 02-16-2015, 11:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/444-dessembrae","name":"Dessembrae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar444_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"362"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-16T08:15:27-08:00","id":407775,"title":"","content":"Typically, what I do as a GM, is not force the players to do something with a roll. I don't believe in allowing a PC to be mind controlled by an NPC, because of a die roll. So the only time I have NPCs roll during social combat is when they are trying to mind control other NPCs or when they are lying to the PCs. If they are mind controlling the NPCs I set it up so that the players get a chance to also mind control the NPCs. When they are lying to the players, the players are entitled to their roll to see through the lie. If they don't make it, I tell them the lie again. If they do, they find out they were lying. <br /> <br /> As for the supernatural mind control things, if a player does not make the resistance to not be actually mind controlled then I, the evil heartless GM that I am, take control of their character. I've found that by doing that, I remove the part about forcing them to play compliant. When I do that though, I don't take the sheet so while I know the basics of what the character can do, I have no more knowledge of specifics than does the NPC doing the mind controlling.<br /> <br /> I hope that helps you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Wow I wish I had something cool to say here</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2227-dman1123","name":"dman1123","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-16T11:24:15-08:00","id":407972,"title":"","content":"It's Tricky.... You can Tell them to go along with it but that will make them feel Railroaded.. or you can explain that the NPC's Arguement is compelling enough too make you realise that what he's saying makes sense... Atleast at this exact moment...<br /> <br /> Everyone can suddenly find themselves buying into something they wouldn't normally do when speaking with people.. you only realise that they tricked you AFTER the fact most of the Time. If the PC fails too the same NPC enough i'd give them abit of a bonus for there wariness. Supernatural Complusions should be worse.. just have them suddenly agreeing and going along with it. Just... Don't do it all the Time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Light and Dark are two Sides of a Coin... Humanity is What Happens when it Lands on it's Edge.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3981-rickmanuk","name":"RickmanUK","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3981_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"533"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-16T11:27:50-08:00","id":407974,"title":"","content":"What happens when one side or the other loses a contested social roll is something your group should work out in advance, and something that should be worked out to everyone's comfort. The GMC has the Doors system, and a lot of groups have social &quot;health levels&quot; equal to Willpower...after all, one successful contested hit does not end a fight.<br /> <br /> But it is something that needs to be worked out early, so players can build their characters knowing how the social system works. One where they can be easily manipulated if they fail may lead to more investment in resistance attributes, while one where social rolls mean little can and should result in less investment in social skills.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/17537-v20-the-conspiracy-of-hrad-%C4%8Dern%C3%A1-hora-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Vampire: The Conspiracy of Hrad \u010cern\u00e1 Hora</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/13838-scion-bohemian-front-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Scion: Bohemian Front</a>][<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/99033-changeling-malibu-dream-house-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling: Malibu Dream House</a>] [<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/268069-demon-night-train-detective-agency-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: Night Train Detective Agency</a>] <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/la94fvk\" target=\"_blank\">[WoD: The Golden Eagle</a>]","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/56-baroness-nerak","name":"Baroness Nerak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar56_2.jpeg","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2524"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T06:38:06-08:00","id":408912,"title":"","content":"Thanks!<br /> I really like the idea of giving the player a choice here. First of all a social roll is not mind control, it aims to trigger a response in the character. A success on the NPC side would mean that the player should feel compelled to do what the other wants. As this will be a tool to support storytelling, the conversation will literally take place during game, and the character will be in most cases aware of what\u2019s going. They should decide whether to expand a willpower point to strengthen their composure or resolve if they don't like where the conversation is going.<br /> I intend to modify the rules as follows:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>If they expanded a willpower point during the struggle they lost and they\u2019re still adamant on not doing what the NPC wants (and in fact their character as well) they can spend another willpower point. This will completely nullify the roll. This especially makes sense if by resisting they submit to their vice, in this case the net willpower cost will be one, providing they will roleplay it right.</li> <li>They can ignore effect of a roll simply by submitting to denial. But this is a sin against clarity one way or another.<br /> Let\u2019s say that a rival (NPC) appeared on their (PCs) doorstep and brought his both Rottweilers along. He mentioned something about paying up, while the doggies chewed on a meaty bone. These two got him quite a few bonus dice to the roll. The NPC was successful in intimidating. Our hero however blinked a few times and saw only two cute pugs squeaking a rubber toy. Gorgeous little things. He chose the denial road stepping back from Clarity.<br /> <br /> To make the story more interesting characters with clarity above 0 will later on second guess their perspective on the situation. The images of the Rottweiler\u2019s bloody teeth will come back and haunt them for a while (flashbacks, barking in the Hedge or during dreamshaping?). It will be up to them to somehow fix this situation and get back the grip on reality.<br /> Still they will always have a choice to forget about the whole issue and just roll a degeneration roll. This will also happen if they stall and ignore these thoughts for too long.</li> </ol>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5073-casualmoth","name":"CasualMoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5073_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T12:11:57-08:00","id":409194,"title":"","content":"If I may, ignoring a social roll as a Breaking Point is something that really rubs me the wrong way. I don't feel like it makes good sense nor good gameplay. It both makes social tests largely useless and reduces Clarity to a resource for ignoring a part of the game the player doesn't like without addressing why players tend to dislike it.<br /> <br /> Often I find the reason people dislike social tests is because it takes away their choice about how their character behaves. You can get around this by just letting PC's chose to ignore social tests but that's not very satisfying for players (such as yourself) who want social pressure to be a thing.<br /> <br /> Instead I would recommend a system that gives social tests teeth that can't be ignored but still gives the characters choices in which direction to go. As a quick and dirty solution:<br /> <br /> \u2022 A social test cannot make a character violate their moral code. If the actions asked of them would be a Breaking Point the character can instead perform a compromise that would only be a violation at one Clarity higher then they currently are. For example, a Clarity 4 changeling asked to kidnap a fellow Lost might instead kill the target.<br /> <br /> \u2022 A character can spend Willpower on a one-for-two basis to boost their Clarity for the purpose of this compromise. The above Clarity 4 character might burn 2 Willpower to boost their effective Clarity to 8 and only injure the target.<br /> <br /> This would give some good incentive to keep Clarity high and gives characters tools to choose how to respond to social sway without being able to outright ignore it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4692,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4692-helel","user":"helel","date":". 02-17-2015, 03:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4692-helel","name":"helel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-18T01:52:44-08:00","id":409798,"title":"","content":"In PC -&gt; NPC interaction or the other way around I can't really see a simple situation, where a single roll would have an effect of forcing a Breaking Point. It would be possible if you are in a unhealthy close relationship where the other side is submissive, or you're manipulating a extremely mentally unstable person. In other words it would be a very complicated and extended on the storytelling side action. Even if you're mid-powered Ventrue vampire it wouldn't be that simple.<br /> In my view a social roll triggers a strong emotion a person feels compelled to follow. However even if we consider making a roll, there would be common sense involved first. If the guy intimidating you is 2 feet tall, has a speech defect he'd better have some good tools or a scenery set up to scare you. Cold iron bolted crossbow aimed at your fellow changeling, or a very good blackmail leverage, or situation where you're threatened by your bane. There have to be some sound requirements to be met before we consider a roll and bonus/penalty dice. If the common sense tells us that it wouldn't be possible to persuade/lie/intimidate in a certain way we just say no to a roll. If the PC wants to jump over a 30 story building you don't give them a -5 on athletics, you just say no.<br /> As overly criticizing I may sound I'm just defending the idea I really like and try to explain it better. A successful roll makes the character strongly feel in a certain way. A way of overcoming this compulsion to comply introduces a cost of losing a social challenge but allows the player to take initiative while still feeling the blow.<br /> <br /> Edit:<br /> I just started reading the GMC rules update so apologies for using some of the terms like 'Breaking Point' in incorrect way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5073,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5073-casualmoth","user":"CasualMoth","date":". 02-18-2015, 06:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5073-casualmoth","name":"CasualMoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5073_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-18T06:41:27-08:00","id":410039,"title":"","content":"I think I'm not clear on what exactly you're having social rolls do that your players have difficulty with then. Normally the problem is &quot;some NPC rolled the dice and now I'm forced to do action X&quot; but you're making it sound more like you're limiting yourself to &quot;you can't tell the npc is lying&quot; or &quot;the npc is very scary, play accordingly.&quot; That's a whole lesser ball game of social influence.<br /> <br /> For example, lets say a low life ogre NPC wants the PC's to help get rid of a drug dealer on the corner. I thought you were doing something like &quot;ogre rolls intimidation and wins so you're all scared enough to go along with his request and take out that dealer.&quot; What would the social test and successful result actually look like in your game?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4692-helel","name":"helel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-18T09:10:34-08:00","id":410155,"title":"","content":"I would suggest using the GMC Conditions system for this. If an NPC beats a PC with an Intimidation Roll they get the the Intimidated condition, something like -2 to all social rolls involving the one who intimidated them. Resolution: Do what the person wants. This incentivizes the player to play along and make the story better without it being a forced thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-18T09:54:26-08:00","id":410250,"title":"","content":"Another way of handeling these situations - which has the downside of making you look alittle petty and unfair while also forcing you to potentially change's on the fly - is to simply have whatever outcome the players end of doing, serve your antagonist in some fashion. Say that they're a bunch of Hunters who're dealing with a Mage. He pleads with the Hunters not to go after the Vampire's down by the slums, because they're his allies, in spite of everything they've done. The Hunters dismiss the Mage, and go after the Hunters anyway, in spite of their failed social rolls against the Mage.<br /> Loe and behold, when they stand triumphantly over the Vampires (if that is even the outcome), it becomes aparent that the Mage actually wanted them to take out the Vampires because their Haven were located on top of a magical nexus, which him and his Cabal now has free access to. Thanks Hunters.<br /> <br /> Of course if it was paramount - or just a very ingrained fact - in your story that the Mages were allied to this particular Coterie, then that makes this approach much harder to pull off.<br /> <br /> I usually handle this with a few discreet nods and prods. Say I have an NPC who is selling a new drug, and the PC in question owns a drug palor. Instead of laying emphasis on how the gentleman in question seems to wrap his words in honey and silk, specifically emphasising things that the PC in question would be pleased to hear and that he gives off an eerie wibe and smell of sex and sulfur, I'll instead emphasise how the new drug sounds really amazing, and will help the PC's economy - which has been rather failing recently, after all. I'll emphasise all the reasons why the PC should be willing and happy to make this deal, instead of all the reasons he might not, or even the reasons why it might be a neutral idea. If the PC lost the roll, that means he doesn't pick up on the reasons it might be anything but unquestioningly good. Thats the intend of the NPC after all. To emphasise why its a good idea, and to obsfucate all the reasons why it might not be a good choise. If he still wants to decline the deal, knowing only that the deal is unquestioningly in his favor, thats his own choise.<br /> Of course I'll probably remind him a few times in the comming days/weeks, how if he had made that deal, he could've bought that gold watch he coveted, or how that woman who turned him down, might've been willing to go out with him if he had been wearing that expensive suit that he could've bought, had he only made the deal.<br /> Thats how life works, after all. If you turn down something that you cant really see the detrimental effects of - probably because you're afraid of the abrupt change it could bring to your life, and you're afraid of change - you're going to be thinking about it for a while, everytime you encounter something that might've been different, if you've made that decision.<br /> <br /> I think taking it as far as assigning a Breaking Point to this kind of thing as a general rule, is alittle tough, but I can totally see it happening in specific cases.<br /> I certainly remember being torn up over not having made a few choises in my own life and I consider myself a relatively grounded individual.<br /> <br /> You could also veto that the failure of the players in that social encounter, means that they accidentally promise the NPC that they'll comply with his wishes, while they're in the room with him, and afterwards once they're out of there, they start thinking of what it actually was they agreed to do, and decide against it (perhaps), thereby betraying their promise to the NPC, which can be kinda dangerous in some cases.<br /> I think its generally a good idea to think less of mechanical consequences, and more of narrative ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1958,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1958-krobeles","user":"Krobeles","date":". 02-18-2015, 12:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1958-krobeles","name":"Krobeles","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1958_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-19T14:18:45-08:00","id":412072,"title":"","content":"I certainly tend to veer away from contested social rolls, in any system. Not in a conscious way, really: I usually just trust my players to act according to their characters during role-played dialogue.<br /> <br /> Here's how I see the problem: it's the difference between active and passive statements. The standard mechanical approach to, say, an NPC trying to persuade a PC is to make some defended or contested rolls and, if the roll succeeds, the PC is persuaded. The subject of the roll, effectively, is the PC: the player now needs to incorporate that into his/her portrayal of the character, probably with the full OOC knowledge that they're being manipulated.<br /> <br /> Here's how I tend to do it, instead. The NPC wishes to persuade the PC. I don't make the player roll anything: I roll for the NPC, subtracting suitable modifiers. Based on the successes of that roll, I tailor my portrayal of the NPC and their rhetoric to match. A failed roll means stuttering and badly conveyed arguments. I high roll means utter conviction and clear, valid arguments. The subject of this roll is the NPC, not the PC.<br /> <br /> The players can then *choose* whether to be persuaded or not, based on their own judgement. The roll just acts as a guide for how to play the character for that particular dialogue. Instead of the result being &quot;The PC is charmed,&quot; it becomes &quot;The NPC is charming.&quot;<br /> <br /> As a result, I'm also a fan of letting players roll their social actions before they say what they want to say in character. They can then tailor their mannerisms and dialogue accordingly. Little deflates a player more than improvising an awesome, moving speech only to have the dice throw up a botch.<br /> <br /> Maybe this is all obvious stuff. It made me reflect on how I run social encounters, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4722-birnam","name":"Birnam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4722_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"42"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-20T01:26:37-08:00","id":412553,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>helel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/407731-pc-losing-a-social-contested-roll-dilemma?p=410039#post410039\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I'm not clear on what exactly you're having social rolls do that your players have difficulty with then. Normally the problem is &quot;some NPC rolled the dice and now I'm forced to do action X&quot; but you're making it sound more like you're limiting yourself to &quot;you can't tell the npc is lying&quot; or &quot;the npc is very scary, play accordingly.&quot; That's a whole lesser ball game of social influence.<br /> <br /> For example, lets say a low life ogre NPC wants the PC's to help get rid of a drug dealer on the corner. I thought you were doing something like &quot;ogre rolls intimidation and wins so you're all scared enough to go along with his request and take out that dealer.&quot; What would the social test and successful result actually look like in your game?</div> </div> </div> </div>The way I imagined social rolls would be 'Opening Doors' in GMC terms. Not the full roll and result, just the emotions and thoughts you produce.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5073-casualmoth","name":"CasualMoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5073_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-20T02:26:05-08:00","id":412580,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Krobeles</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/407731-pc-losing-a-social-contested-roll-dilemma?p=410250#post410250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Thats the intend of the NPC after all. To emphasise why its a good idea, and to obsfucate all the reasons why it might not be a good choise. If he still wants to decline the deal, knowing only that the deal is unquestioningly in his favor, thats his own choise.<br /> Of course I'll probably remind him a few times in the coming days/weeks, how if he had made that deal, he could've bought that gold watch he coveted, or how that woman who turned him down, might've been willing to go out with him if he had been wearing that expensive suit that he could've bought, had he only made the deal.<br /> Thats how life works, after all. If you turn down something that you cant really see the detrimental effects of - probably because you're afraid of the abrupt change it could bring to your life, and you're afraid of change - you're going to be thinking about it for a while, everytime you encounter something that might've been different, if you've made that decision.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's how I would do it in the past, but in the end, we would all see that the social rolls are not really important. Nobody really treated them seriously. We all fell back to the notion, that the storytelling is only important. Social rolls were only used to harvest Vitae or Glamour, sometimes to spot a lie, as a different perception roll.<br /> That's perfectly OK. However I always felt that some part of the character sheet was kind of useless. When the players presented me their PC draft, they would usually point out how much they invested in the social part, to show me that they\u2019re mature role-players, and that they're not really interested in power gaming. If they focused on powers and combat I would ask them to invest more in social mental part.<br /> \u2026<br /> I consider this a flaw of my GM\u2019ing and I will change it somehow in the upcoming campaign. Having players act according to social rolls, let's say in GMC way would be quite interesting. They will know that the NPC works to open their Doors (Social Manoeuvring) and will be able to act accordingly. I will allow them to instantly fail opponents Door opening attempt, if they fall back to Clarity based denial. Depending on severity of this denial, I will add a Condition that penalizes their perception and social rolls until they role play it back to reality; AND in a serious case ask for an instant breaking point roll. If the NPC initiated some kind of negotiation that taken weeks and a few Doors were opened and the PC decides suddenly that HE is The Great Bear of the Hedge and mortal contracts do not apply to him, it will definitely cost them Integrity/Clarity (in this case it would be at least -3 BP roll).<br /> <br /> You fellow GMs might ask why take so much interest in this ?<br /> Because I know that my players in some part treat the campaign also as a board game night. They love their stats not as much as the stories and dangers they experienced but they still love them. They\u2019d really like to feel physically, in game mechanic terms, that their character is just good at something, because the rolls, not me saiyng so. The best way to do it is playing on equal (as far as they know) terms with NPCs, not only in combat.<br /> One more thing is that this will provide a reasonable source of story arcs and randomness that will surprise me. The player might feel like: \u201cI\u2019m in a mood to play with my intimidation tonight\u201d (remembering how fun it was to use Social Manoeuvring) but in fact, he\u2019ll take the story into his own hands and maybe gain some new enemies for me to play with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5073,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5073-casualmoth","user":"CasualMoth","date":". 02-20-2015, 05:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5073-casualmoth","name":"CasualMoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5073_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-20T07:14:59-08:00","id":412712,"title":"","content":"If you're going with GMC's updated social mechanics then there is ALREADY a method baked in to provide the PC's protection from social orders they don't want to comply with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>God Machine Chronicles, pg 193</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:georgia\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Go With the Flow</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:georgia\">If the character does as requested and abides by the intended goal, his player takes a Beat <span style=\"color:#1A43AE\">(see p. 157)</span>.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:georgia\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Offer an Alternative</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:georgia\">If the subject\u2019s player chooses, he may offer a beneficial alternative and the initiator\u2019s player can impose a Condition <span style=\"color:#1A43AE\">(see p. 180) </span>on his character. This offer exists between players; it does not need to occur within the fiction of the game (though it can). The alternative must be beneficial and not a twist of intent. The Storyteller adjudicates. </span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:GoudyOldStyleT\"><span style=\"font-family:georgia\">The initiator\u2019s player chooses a Condition to impose on the subject. It must make sense within the context of the scenario. </span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>If you're using GMC opening doors -&gt; social action complete in X doors you might as well use their &quot;Do the thing, get a beat&quot; mechanic as well. It's much better then &quot;Do the thing or risk going insane.&quot;<br /> <br /> If you don't use Beats then just give a Willpower point instead and make offering an alternative <i>cost</i> a Willpower point. A net difference of 2 Willpower is enough to make players generally just go along with the request but gives the option to fight it and go for compromise when they feel strongly that their characters would never to &quot;the thing.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4692,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4692-helel","user":"helel","date":". 02-20-2015, 10:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4692-helel","name":"helel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T02:12:42-08:00","id":414997,"title":"","content":"The simplest solution is the best here. I think you might be right. I'll go with GMC way, my players are quite hyped about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5073-casualmoth","name":"CasualMoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5073_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T06:10:48-08:00","id":415079,"title":"","content":"For me a roll of any sort should always have any outcome it can have be interesting and fun in some way. If winning or losing a dice roll would result in a super lame unfun outcome, don't roll.<br /> <br /> So, that brings us to social rolls. Same deal. Only roll if failure is a good fun possibility. For PCs influencing NPCs, it's the ST's choice, and I find it pretty easy to figure out. Just think, &quot;could this go either way?&quot; and &quot;would either way be fun?&quot; But for NPCs influencing PCs, you don't really know if the PC in question would find either result fun. So, consider it yourself, then ask. Say, &quot;How about a roll to figure out if you buy this argument?&quot; And if they agree, roll it and let the dice decide. If they don't ask why, and maybe all that's needed is are tweaked possible results.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/11I0xCPgAS3ZhxGvTlBoePOuaDqgpyWs13jVKhJ4c8hM/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Book of the Emerald Circle</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/37100-white-elephant-for-flintlock-sidereal-custom-charms-and-discussion-on-themes\" target=\"_blank\">Custom Sidereal Charms</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sQxyi3ox0qn53c_vsUaVQi_pRdpKlQE50NT5ldQmxYI/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Expanded Sidereal Linguistics</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/263-limited-reagent","name":"Limited Reagent","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar263_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"591"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T07:05:50-08:00","id":415101,"title":"","content":"Well, if the problem is that players are trying to get back at NPCs that used social maneuvers (or even outright mind control powers) against them, then that's not really much of a problem.<br /> <br /> Players SHOULD be vulnerable to certain things. If a huge, scary thug intimidates them, it should have the chance to make them buckle, rather than do whatever they want. If they then feel indignant about their apparent moment of cowardice, and want to take revenge from a safe hiding place where they think they won't have to pay any consequence for doing so, then that's a pretty human reaction.<br /> <br /> Players might also be convinced of something by a con man, only to realize what a bill of goods they just got sold, and go for some subtle form of revenge, as well. Think of a child that thinks they have to have some random cheap toy sold at exorbitant prices, only to find it doesn't do anything they dreamed it would at the start. They throw the toy away as soon as they've played it once, and never think of it again.<br /> <br /> Outside of long-term brainwashing, there's good reason to just talk a player into doing something against their nature because their character is being talked into doing something against their nature, only to have them resent the action, and go back against the person who manipulated them as soon as they are outside the influence of the social pressures that forced them to crack.<br /> <br /> That said, it's perfectly fine to judge someone on how petty their spats of revenge are, as well, and impose clarity breaks if someone treats being talked into helping a buddy pack up their stuff and move as a personal insult that must be avenged with magical social humiliation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CasualMoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/407731-pc-losing-a-social-contested-roll-dilemma?p=412580#post412580\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I consider this a flaw of my GM\u2019ing and I will change it somehow in the upcoming campaign. Having players act according to social rolls, let's say in GMC way would be quite interesting. They will know that the NPC works to open their Doors (Social Manoeuvring) and will be able to act accordingly. I will allow them to instantly fail opponents Door opening attempt, if they fall back to Clarity based denial. Depending on severity of this denial, I will add a Condition that penalizes their perception and social rolls until they role play it back to reality; AND in a serious case ask for an instant breaking point roll. If the NPC initiated some kind of negotiation that taken weeks and a few Doors were opened and the PC decides suddenly that HE is The Great Bear of the Hedge and mortal contracts do not apply to him, it will definitely cost them Integrity/Clarity (in this case it would be at least -3 BP roll).</div> </div> </div> </div>If the problem is one of perception then, really, why not just make your social doors the hit points of an alternative combat, thematically? If the problem is that they see combat and health boxes as the &quot;real things to worry about&quot;, and don't see social rolls as serious because they're less structured, then just make those social doors into hit points in theme, and social combat a fully viable alternate form of combat. Talk about it with the same terms (&quot;the insult slams into your gut, breaking another two of your doors, and the snickers of those around, enjoying watching you cut down from your lofty perch inflict a further condition that gives you a -2 to your next roll...&quot;) Describe those doors in terms of pain or desperation that mounts with each hit point they bleed out, or in the case of using honey rather than vinegar, just start outright being an unreliable narrator of events, so that the player doesn't even know about the downsides to things their character is being persuaded towards doing.<br /> <br /> There's no reason you can't have the party &quot;face&quot; have a life-or-death social duel with a rival over the fate of a Court's direction the same way they might have an honor duel with rapiers, but one of the big problems with a single Charisma stat and a &quot;face&quot; in many games is that it inherently means the others sit out. Organize social challenges like you organize fights, and let other players tackle different challenges. One player may be the &quot;DPS&quot; of a social battle, pushing your goals offensively with persuasion and manipulation, while another may be a &quot;support&quot;, spotting the lies and tactics of the other side with empathy and wits, and the brute of the party blocks off enemy support with a swift intimidation roll to keep third parties that might support your opponents shut up in the sidelines.<br /> <br /> Make social maneuvers all but literal combat maneuvers, and play it up as strike and riposte, with strategies on either side. (To this end, I dislike the way that GMC treats door-opening as things that take place over weeks because of some stiff, arbitrary rules. The time scale should be whatever is appropriate to the drama.) Then, when they lose all their doors, you're justified in saying that you're taking their player out of their hands, the way that you can't play your character when unconscious or dead. They lost the fight, so they buckled, and aren't in control, anymore. Fear of that sort of loss of control should be more potent a motivator than an experience beat bribe, the way people don't screw around when the guns come out. If you make social doors apply to anyone (as it is a reflection of a more fragile state of mind) until &quot;healed&quot;, then you should be able to complete the metaphor of hit points and combat, entirely.<br /> <br /> Think of it like how some RPGs have characters with high physical DEF but low magical MDF. Rather than tell your players that &quot;they're not being serious role-players&quot; when they don't invest in social skills, imply that they're leaving themselves vulnerable to different forms of attack or that they are going to be one-trick ponies that sit out the likely unavoidable social conflicts. You can easily frame problems for Changelings in situations where they can't simply punch the people they disagree with, like Court politics or better yet, dealing with the mundane human social power structures.<br /> <br /> Changelings, especially, are a type of being that thrives more on mental and social skills than the combat skills, anyway. A secret pried from your opponent might be more damaging than a cold iron spear, in the right hands. Forcing a broken pledge can be tantamount to homicide. Players should be willing to consider a stealthy spy with information-gathering skills or a tempter that gets their paramour to &quot;willingly&quot; hand over the knowledge of their greatest weakness just as much a fearsome combatant as a walking lion, and it's one of the few games where you honestly could just throw physical combat skills to the wind, and go completely with mental or social skills with no loss in effectiveness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5120,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","user":"Wraith_Magus","date":". 02-22-2015, 10:08 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","name":"Wraith_Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T19:15:59-08:00","id":426045,"title":"","content":"If you want to continue using social rolls and feel that the players are taking losing rolls as an &quot;attack&quot; so to speak and later seek revenge, simply take it one step further. When they take action against that person bring the weight of the Court down on them. No court monarch is going to think &quot;but she outwitted me&quot; or &quot;he was a little off-putting&quot; as an excuse to take action against another courtier. Would be wonderfully Changeling to then place your characters in the debt of the NPC.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5202-hotchepsoot","name":"HotChepSoot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}